course: dictionary
lesson: 6661
title: Nutrients: what living things need
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A nutrient is a substance that a living thing needs to grow, stay healthy and have energy. Food is full of nutrients, such as protein, vitamins and minerals. «Vegetarians can get all their nutrients from beans, eggs, dairy, nuts, grains and vegetables.» The nutrients are inside the food, so the food is where you find them.
explain: Plants need nutrients too, but they take them from soil, not from a plate. Their roots take in water and nutrients. «Decomposers such as fungi, bacteria and earthworms break down dead matter and return nutrients to the soil.» Nutrients travel in a loop: from soil into plants, and from dead plants back into the soil.
explain: Use the clue words around the word. In «nutrient-rich food», rich means having a lot of something. So nutrient-rich food has lots of the substances your body needs. Check by swapping in your meaning: "food with lots of useful substances" still makes sense.
fact: A nutrient is a substance that a living thing needs.
fact: The main nutrients for people are carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre and water.
fact: Food labels list the nutrients in the food.
fact: Essential nutrients are ones the body needs but cannot make in enough amounts, so it must get them from food.
fact: «Whale poo spreads nutrients that feed plankton, and plankton makes much of Earth's oxygen.»
exercise: What is a nutrient? | *A substance in food that a living thing needs to grow, stay healthy and have energy | A tool used to cook food | A kind of plant that grows in water | A metal that is found in rocks
exercise: «On the warm, wet floor, the trees take the nutrients straight back.» Where do plants take nutrients from? | From the sea | From the air only | *From the soil | From sunlight alone
exercise: «Vegetarians can get all their nutrients from beans, eggs, dairy, nuts, grains and vegetables.» Which of these is not on the list? | Eggs | Beans | *Chicken | Nuts
test: In «nutrient-rich food», what does rich mean? | Expensive and hard to buy | Cooked for a long time | Cold and fresh | *Full of, having a lot of something
test: Which of these is a main nutrient that people need? | *Protein | Smoke | Gravel | Sand
exam: Why might a doctor suggest a multivitamin for very fussy eaters? | Because doctors like giving tablets | *Because they may miss some nutrients | Because vitamins taste nice and sweet | Because fussy eaters never need food
